    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Stable sustained VT:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " IV amiodarone is often used but works slowly (if lidocaine is ineffective, IV procainamide may be given but may take up to 1 hour); if they fail, cardiovert. Long-term: after sustained VT with no transient or reversible cause, patients typically require an ICD; if an ICD can't be used, amiodarone may be preferred; to prevent recurrences BB first, then sotalol, then amiodarone."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Oral amiodarone (recurrent VF/unstable VT): load 800–1600 mg/day for 1–3 weeks, then 600–800 mg/day for 1 month, then usually 400 mg/day; start under hospital or specialist supervision."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Correct K, Mg and Ca before starting; repeat history, exam and CXR every 3–6 months; stop or reduce if transaminases exceed three times normal."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "Interactions (persist weeks–months after stopping): warfarin—PT rises ~100% in 3–4 days, cut warfarin by ⅓–½; digoxin—halve the dose or stop; simvastatin ≤20 mg; other QT-prolonging drugs—avoid (torsades); sofosbuvir—symptomatic bradycardia."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Amiodarone CI:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " cardiogenic shock; sick sinus syndrome, second- or third-degree AV block or bradycardia with syncope without a pacemaker; iodine hypersensitivity."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Counsel:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " photosensitivity in about 10%; use sunscreen or protective clothing."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Overdose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " monitor rhythm and BP; β-agonist or pacemaker for bradycardia; inotropes/vasopressors for hypotension; not dialyzable."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sotalol dose:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " 80 mg BID, raised by 80 mg/day every 3 days if QTc <500 ms (max 320 mg/day); hospitalize ≥3 days with continuous ECG when starting; lengthen the interval by CrCl."
     }
    ]
   },
   {
    "type": "paragraph",
    "attrs": {
     "indLeft": 18,
     "indRight": 0,
     "indFirst": -18,
     "spaceBefore": 0,
     "spaceAfter": 0,
     "line": null,
     "align": "left",
     "shade": null,
     "borders": null,
     "marker": {
      "text": "•",
      "font": null,
      "marks": [],
      "tabPt": 18
     }
    },
    "content": [
     {
      "type": "text",
      "marks": [
       {
        "type": "bold"
       }
      ],
      "text": "Sotalol CI:"
     },
     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": " sinus bradycardia, SSS or second- or third-degree AV block without a pacemaker; long QT; cardiogenic shock or decompensated HF; K <4 mEq/L; asthma/bronchospasm; for AF/AFL also baseline QT >450 ms. Correct low K/Mg first."
     }
    ]
   }
  ]
